course: biology
lesson: 66
title: Lungs and breathing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every cell in your body needs oxygen from the air, and your lungs are where the air and the blood meet.
explain: Lie on the grass and watch your tummy rise and fall: a dome-shaped muscle under your lungs, the diaphragm, pulls down, the chest gets roomier, and air rushes in through your nose.
explain: The air goes down the windpipe into branching tubes like an upside-down tree, ending in hundreds of millions of tiny bubbles where oxygen slips into the blood and carbon dioxide slips out.
explain: Breathe on a cold window and the mist shows that the air you breathe out is warm and wet from inside you.
explain: Your nose cleans the air, a sneeze blasts out dust, and the same breath makes your voice when it buzzes past your vocal cords; whales breathe air too, but fish take their oxygen from water with gills.
fact: The lungs are two spongy organs in the chest; a child's lungs hold about 2 to 3 litres of air and an adult's about 6 litres.
fact: The left lung is a little smaller than the right to make room for the heart.
fact: Breathed-out air is warm and full of water vapour, which turns into tiny droplets of mist on cold glass.
fact: The diaphragm, a dome-shaped muscle under the lungs, pulls down when you breathe in, making more room in the chest so air rushes in; a hiccup is a sudden jerk of the diaphragm.
fact: The air tubes end in about 300 to 500 million tiny air sacs called alveoli, wrapped in capillaries, where oxygen passes into the blood and carbon dioxide comes out.
fact: Spread out flat, all the alveoli would cover an area roughly the size of half a tennis court, a huge surface for oxygen to pass into the blood.
fact: When you run, your muscles need more oxygen and make more carbon dioxide; the brain senses the extra carbon dioxide and makes you breathe faster and deeper, and you keep panting for a while after you stop, until the extra carbon dioxide is out and the oxygen is made up.
fact: The nose warms and moistens air, and its hairs and sticky mucus catch dust and germs before they reach the lungs.
fact: A sneeze is a reflex that blasts air out of the nose and mouth to clear something irritating, like dust or pepper.
fact: Nobody knows for sure why we yawn; one idea is that it helps cool the brain, and yawns are catching, so seeing someone yawn often makes you yawn.
fact: Your voice is made when air from the lungs passes the vocal cords in the voice box, the larynx, making them vibrate; the mouth and tongue shape the sound into words.
fact: Cigarette smoke damages the tiny hairs that clean the airways and the alveoli and leaves sticky tar in the lungs, which is why smoking is banned near playgrounds in many places.
fact: Whales breathe air through a blowhole on top of the head; the blow is warm breath and spray, and some whales can hold their breath for over an hour.
fact: Fish take oxygen from water with gills, thin feathery parts full of blood vessels; water goes in through the mouth and out over the gills, and out of water the gills collapse, so the fish cannot get oxygen.
exercise: Why do we need to breathe? | Our cells need oxygen from the air | To cool down the stomach after every meal | To keep our bones hard and strong | To fill our blood with tiny bits of smoke
exercise: Why does breath make mist on a cold window? | Warm wet breath turns into tiny water droplets on the cold glass | Breath has a little smoke in it that sticks to the glass and stays | The glass starts to sweat whenever a warm person comes near | Your teeth spray fine drops of spit all over the window
exercise: About how much air can a child's lungs hold? | About 2 to 3 litres | About one small teaspoon | About 50 litres | About a whole bathful
exercise: Why is the left lung a little smaller than the right? | To make room for the heart | Because we breathe much more with the right one | Because the stomach squashes it from below | Because it grows later than the right
exercise: What does the diaphragm do when you breathe in? | It pulls down so the chest has more room | It pushes the old air out through the nose | It closes the windpipe so no food gets in | It squeezes the heart to make it beat
exercise: What is a hiccup? | A sudden jerk of the diaphragm | A burp that comes out the wrong way | A tiny cough down in the stomach | A bubble of air stuck in the ears
exercise: What are alveoli? | Hundreds of millions of tiny air sacs in the lungs | The two big tubes that carry the air into each of the lungs | The strong muscles that lie between the ribs | The little hairs inside the nose that catch dust
exercise: What happens in the alveoli? | Oxygen goes into the blood and carbon dioxide comes out | Food from the stomach soaks into the blood through their thin walls | New blood is made there out of the air we breathe | The air we breathe in is turned into water
exercise: Why do you breathe fast and hard after a race? | Your muscles need more oxygen and make more carbon dioxide | Your lungs shrank while you were running and must fill up again | You swallowed too much air and need to get rid of it | Sweat has blocked your nose so you have to pant through the mouth
exercise: What do the hairs and mucus in your nose do? | Catch dust and germs before they reach the lungs | Help you smell sweets and cakes from far away | Keep your nose warm so it does not freeze | Make your breath smell nice and fresh all day
exercise: Why do you sneeze in a dusty attic? | A reflex blasts air out to clear dust from your nose | Dust makes your lungs too full, so they push it all back out | Your nose is allergic to the dark under the roof | Sneezing blows the dust out of the whole room
exercise: Why do we yawn? | Nobody knows for sure; one idea is to cool the brain | Because we have completely run out of air | To stretch the tongue after a long day of talking | Because the jaw muscles are tired and need a rest
exercise: Where is your voice made? | In the voice box, where air makes the vocal cords vibrate | In the lungs, where the air rattles against the ribs as it goes | In the nose, which whistles when the air goes through | In the stomach, which rumbles and sends the sound up
exercise: Why is smoking banned near playgrounds? | Smoke damages the lungs and leaves tar in them | Smoke makes the metal swings go rusty very fast | Smoke scares away the birds that live in the park | Smoke makes the grass grow too fast to cut
exercise: How does a whale breathe? | Through a blowhole on top of its head | With gills on its sides like a fish | Through its skin while it is under water | Through little holes in its tail fin
exercise: How does a goldfish get oxygen? | Its gills take oxygen from the water | It swims to the top to gulp air every second | It breathes in through its fins and tail | It does not need any oxygen at all
test: shut up and blow, how come one breath can blow up a whole balloon? | Your lungs hold a few litres of air and you push most of it out | The balloon sucks the air out of you by itself once you start blowing | Breath swells up to ten times bigger once it is warm | Your stomach blows extra air into the balloon too
test: In a cartoon a fish lies on the beach and breathes fine for an hour. Put it right. | Out of water a fish's gills collapse and it cannot get oxygen | Fish can breathe air just as well as water, so the cartoon is fine | Fish do not need to breathe at all, so nothing goes wrong | Fish can hold their breath for days, so an hour is easy
test: My brother says whales are fish because they live in the sea. Right? | No, whales breathe air with lungs through a blowhole | Yes, they have gills hidden just under the blowhole | Yes, the blow is the water coming out of their gills | No, whales are really a kind of giant shark
test: What would happen if your nose had no hairs or mucus? | More dust and germs would reach your lungs | You could smell everything much better | You would sneeze every single second | Nothing at all would change for you
test: The whole class yawns one after another. What does that show? | Yawns are catching, though nobody knows exactly why | The classroom has run out of air for everyone | Everyone in the class is getting ill with flu | Yawns are a sign that everyone is very hungry
test: lol why does my throat buzz when i hum? | Air is making your vocal cords vibrate | There is a bee stuck in your throat lol | Your lungs are rattling against your ribs | Your teeth are humming along with you
test: Lily breathes faster after running even though she stopped. Why does it keep going for a bit? | Her body still has extra carbon dioxide to breathe out and oxygen to make up | Her lungs forgot to slow down and need a reminder from her brain and her teacher | Her heart is still pushing air into her lungs as fast as it can | Her nose got blocked during the race so she has to gasp
test: hiccups again!! my nan says it's my lungs hiccuping. right? | No, a hiccup is a sudden jerk of the diaphragm under the lungs | Yes, the lungs hop up and down inside your chest | Yes, the lungs are coughing because air went the wrong way | No, it is your heart skipping a beat every few seconds
test: Why does a whale's blow look like a fountain on a cold day? | Its warm, wet breath turns to mist in the cold air, with some spray | The whale is spouting out the seawater it swallowed into its stomach | The whale is sneezing out the little fish it could not eat | The whale is blowing a big jet of bubbles out of its tail
test: In a game your hero holds his breath for an hour underwater. Which animal could really do that? | Some whales | Humans who train hard | Goldfish | Pet dogs
test: Why is the inside of the lungs made of millions of tiny sacs instead of one big bag? | Many tiny sacs give a huge surface for oxygen to pass into blood | Tiny sacs are much lighter for the body to carry around all day long | One big bag would get far too cold in the winter | Tiny sacs make the voice louder when you shout or sing
exam: Identify the principal function of the alveoli of the lungs. | The exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide | The production of the voice by vibration | The filtration of dust from inspired air | The storage of food reserves for the body
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the action of the diaphragm during inspiration? | It contracts and descends, enlarging the chest cavity | It ascends and compresses the lungs from below and behind | It closes the windpipe against the entry of food | It remains wholly inactive throughout inspiration
exam: It is given that a person has engaged in vigorous running. Ascertain the principal stimulus for the consequent increase in rate of breathing. | A rise in carbon dioxide detected by the brain | A reduction in the size of the lungs during exertion | The cooling of the blood by the evaporation of sweat | The obstruction of the nasal passages by mucus
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the respiration of whales. | Whales breathe air by means of lungs through a blowhole | Whales extract oxygen from sea water by means of gills | Whales respire through the skin alone while submerged | Whales do not require oxygen at any point in their lives
